<div class="article-body">
    <Fragment set:html={`<h2>Why Mobility Matters for Dead Hangs</h2>
        <p>A dead hang needs full overhead shoulder flexion and adequate wrist extension. Some adults, especially those who sit for long periods, have limited overhead range. Tight lats, a stiff thoracic spine and short pectoral muscles can all restrict the overhead position. Limited range may force compensation when you hang.</p>
        <p>Arching the lower back is a common way to fake overhead reach. Better shoulder mobility makes it easier to hang in a straight line from hands to hips.</p>
        <p>Stiff wrists change how force is spread across the hand. Wrist drills may ease wrist discomfort for some people.</p>
        <p>A warm-up helps your grip and shoulders feel ready; evidence that it prevents injury is limited. The routine below takes 2 to 3 minutes. The rest of this page covers longer mobility work for rest days.</p>

        <h2>Pre-Hang Warm-Up Routine</h2>
        <p>This 2 to 3 minute routine is the single dead hang warm-up used across this site. Perform the four steps in order before your first working set. It is common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p>

        <h3>1. Arm Circles</h3>
        <p>Stand with arms at your sides. Make small forward circles with both arms at once. Gradually increase the circle size over 15 repetitions until you reach full-range overhead circles. Reverse direction for 15 backward circles.</p>

        <h3>2. Wrist Circles</h3>
        <p>Interlace your fingers and rotate both wrists together in large, slow circles. Perform 10 circles in each direction. Control the end range in every direction.</p>

        <h3>3. Band Pull-Aparts</h3>
        <p>Hold a light resistance band at chest height with straight arms, shoulder-width apart. Pull the band apart by drawing your shoulder blades together. Return slowly to the start position. Perform 15 to 20 repetitions. Band pull-aparts work the rhomboids, lower trapezius and posterior deltoids, which stabilize the shoulder blades during a hang.</p>

        <h3>4. One or Two Short Easy Hangs</h3>
        <p>Hang for a few seconds at a comfortable effort, well short of any fatigue. Hold the bar, let the shoulders settle and step down. This lets the grip and shoulders meet bodyweight before the working sets.</p>

        <div class="callout">
          <h3>Quick-Reference Warm-Up</h3>
          <ol>
            <li>Arm circles: 15 forward and 15 backward</li>
            <li>Wrist circles: 10 in each direction</li>
            <li>Band pull-aparts: 15 to 20 reps</li>
            <li>One or two short easy hangs</li>
          </ol>
          <p>Total time: 2 to 3 minutes. Perform before a dead hang session.</p>
        </div>

        <h2>Shoulder Mobility Drills</h2>
        <p>These shoulder drills target specific shoulder restrictions. Add one or two to your warm-up, or do them as a standalone mobility session on rest days.</p>

        <h3>Wall Slides</h3>
        <p>Stand with your entire back pressed against a wall. Pin your elbows and wrists to the wall throughout the movement. Slide your arms from a 90-degree position to full overhead extension. If your arms leave the wall at any point, that angle marks your current mobility limit. Work at that edge for 10 to 15 reps until you can keep wall contact through the full range.</p>

        <h3>Sleeper Stretch</h3>
        <p>Lie on your side with your bottom arm straight in front at shoulder height. Bend the bottom elbow to 90 degrees. Use your top hand to gently push the bottom forearm toward the floor. Hold for 30 to 45 seconds per side. The sleeper stretch works the back of the shoulder and helps internal rotation. Stop if it pinches.</p>

        <h3>Doorway Stretch</h3>
        <p>Stand in a doorway. Place your forearms on the door frame with elbows at shoulder height. Step one foot forward and lean through the doorway. Hold for 30 to 45 seconds. Raise your elbows to ear height and repeat. This stretches the pectoralis major at two angles.</p>

        <h3>PVC Pass-Throughs (Shoulder Dislocates)</h3>
        <p>Hold a PVC pipe, broomstick or resistance band with a wide grip. Lift it overhead and behind your body in a smooth arc, keeping your elbows straight. Return to the front. Perform 10 to 15 repetitions. Narrow your grip gradually as flexibility improves. A rigid implement gives better end-range control than a band.</p>

        <p>Stop any drill that pinches or hurts in the shoulder. The <a href="/shoulder-health/">shoulder health guide</a> covers shoulders that need extra care.</p>

        <h2>Wrist Mobility Drills</h2>
        <p>These wrist drills target the extension, flexion and rotation ranges that dead hangs use. Wrist circles from the warm-up also belong in this group.</p>

        <h3>Prayer Stretch</h3>
        <p>Press your palms together in front of your chest. Lower your hands toward your waist while keeping the palms pressed together. Stop when you feel a moderate stretch in the wrist flexors. Hold for 20 to 30 seconds. Repeat 3 times.</p>

        <h3>Tabletop Rocks</h3>
        <p>Kneel on the floor. Place your palms flat with fingers pointing forward. Rock your body forward over your wrists until you feel a stretch on the front of the wrists. Hold 2 to 3 seconds. Rock back. Repeat 10 to 15 times. Rotate your fingers to point backward and repeat to stretch the extensors. Tabletop rocks build wrist extension tolerance. Hanging loads the wrist differently. Transfer to hanging is not shown.</p>

        <h3>Finger Extensions</h3>
        <p>Wrap a rubber band around all five fingers of one hand. Spread your fingers apart against the resistance. Hold for 2 seconds. Return. Perform 15 to 20 repetitions per hand. Finger extensions work the extensors that oppose the flexors used in gripping.</p>

        <h2>Post-Hang Stretches</h2>
        <p>Post-hang stretches work the muscles that did the most during a session. They feel good for many people. Stretching does not reliably reduce soreness (Herbert et al., Cochrane review 2011).</p>

        <h3>Forearm Flexor Stretch</h3>
        <p>Extend one arm forward with the palm facing up. Use your other hand to pull the fingers back toward your body. Hold for 20 to 30 seconds per arm. This lengthens the forearm flexors that work during sustained gripping.</p>

        <h3>Cross-Body Shoulder Stretch</h3>
        <p>Pull one arm across your chest with the opposite hand. Hold for 20 to 30 seconds per side. This stretches the posterior deltoid and infraspinatus.</p>

        <h3>Child's Pose</h3>
        <p>Kneel and sit your hips back toward your heels. Reach your arms forward along the floor. Hold for 45 to 60 seconds. Child's pose stretches the latissimus dorsi and the lower back muscles.</p>

        <h3>Cat-Cow</h3>
        <p>Start on hands and knees. Arch your back upward (cat) and hold 2 to 3 seconds. Drop your belly toward the floor and lift your head (cow). Hold 2 to 3 seconds. Alternate for 10 repetitions. Cat-cow moves the thoracic and lumbar spine through flexion and extension.</p>

        <h2>Mobility Routine for Non-Training Days</h2>
        <p>Rest days allow mobility work without the fatigue of a training session. This 10 minute routine is common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p>

        <div class="callout">
          <h3>Rest-Day Mobility Routine (10 Minutes)</h3>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Doorway stretch:</strong> 2 x 30 seconds at each elbow height</li>
            <li><strong>Sleeper stretch:</strong> 2 x 30 seconds per side</li>
            <li><strong>Tabletop wrist rocks:</strong> 15 reps with fingers forward and 15 reps with fingers backward</li>
            <li><strong>PVC pass-throughs:</strong> 15 reps, narrowing grip gradually</li>
            <li><strong>Child's pose:</strong> 60 seconds</li>
            <li><strong>Cat-cow:</strong> 15 repetitions</li>
            <li><strong>Rubber band finger extensions:</strong> 20 reps per hand</li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <p>Many people do this routine 2 to 3 times per week on non-hanging days. Overhead range often improves over several weeks of regular practice, and the time varies. Many people find short, frequent sessions easier to keep up.</p>

        <h2>Self-Checks for Overhead Reach, Wrists and Hang Position</h2>
        <p>These three self-checks are screening heuristics, not diagnostic tests. Repeat them every 4 weeks or so to track your own progress.</p>

        <h3>Overhead Reach Test (Wall Test)</h3>
        <p>Stand with your back flat against a wall. Raise both arms overhead with straight elbows. Try to touch the wall with your thumbs. Pass: thumbs touch the wall without the lower back arching away from the wall. Needs work: thumbs do not reach the wall or the lower back lifts. The test combines shoulder flexion and thoracic extension.</p>

        <h3>Wrist Extension Test</h3>
        <p>Place your palms flat on a table with fingers pointing forward. Lean forward over your wrists. Measure the angle between your forearm and the back of your hand. Normal passive wrist extension is roughly 70 degrees. If you cannot reach 60 degrees, or the test hurts, practice the wrist drills and see a clinician if pain persists.</p>

        <h3>Hang Position Check</h3>
        <p>Hang from a bar and have someone photograph you from the side. Pass: arms, torso and legs form a straight vertical line. Needs work: excessive lower back arch, bent elbows or shoulders that stop short of full overhead. This check shows how your mobility shows up in the hanging position.</p>

