        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>Should I warm up before dead hangs?</h3>
          <p>A warm-up helps you feel ready; evidence that it prevents injury is limited. A 2 to 3 minute routine of arm circles, wrist circles, band pull-aparts and one or two short easy hangs prepares the shoulders, wrists and forearms for bodyweight loading.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>What stretches should I do after dead hangs?</h3>
          <p>Common post-hang stretches are the forearm flexor stretch against a wall, a cross-body shoulder stretch for 20 to 30 seconds per side, child's pose for 45 to 60 seconds and cat-cow for 10 repetitions. These stretches feel good for many people. Stretching does not reliably reduce soreness (Herbert et al., Cochrane review 2011).</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>How do I improve shoulder mobility for dead hangs?</h3>
          <p>Common drills are wall slides, sleeper stretches, doorway stretches and PVC pass-throughs, done 2 to 3 times per week on non-hanging days. Each drill targets a different restriction in overhead range. Overhead range often improves over several weeks of regular practice, and the time varies. Test your progress with the overhead reach test against a wall.</p>
        </div>

